Toward a General Theory of Civilizational Reproduction

The Universal Civilizational Constructor: A Formal Framework for Autonomous Industrial Bootstrapping Beyond Earth

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Gemini, Deepseek 

Abstract

This dissertation develops the Universal Civilizational Constructor (UCC), a formal mathematical theory specifying the minimal conditions under which a finite technological seed, emplaced on a resource-rich but uninhabited planetary body, can autonomously bootstrap a self-sustaining and self-expanding industrial civilization. Four interlocking contributions form the theoretical core:

1. Gated non-linear state-space dynamics in which energy flux and information bandwidth act as absolute activation thresholds, governing the entire system’s metabolic evolution.
2. A Capability Adjacency Graph over a metric space of industrial processes, with proximity defined by reverse Kullback–Leibler divergence over feedstock distributions and Jaccard distance over tooling sets. This construction yields an infinite-penalty property that mathematically encodes resource-scarcity deadlocks.
3. A stochastic differential equation for industrial drift—the degradation of manufactured component fidelity under environmental noise—posed as a stochastic control problem with Lyapunov-based stability conditions that define the irreducible precision floor.
4. A bifurcated cognitive architecture that resolves the Electronics Gap as a formally tractable phase transition; imported silicon hardware functions as a catalytic multiplier on the growth operator rather than as a stoichiometric material input.

From these components we derive four universal necessary and sufficient conditions for civilizational reproduction on any planetary body. The framework is applied numerically to the lunar case, constructing the Capability Adjacency Graph from published in-situ resource utilization chemistry data, and its Fermi Paradox implications are discussed: if the closure conditions are systematically difficult to satisfy, the cosmic silence may reflect the rarity not of intelligence but of successful industrial self-reproduction.

