Part I — Philosophical and Economic Foundations

1. The Framing Error in Settlement Theory

The dominant discourse on interplanetary settlement is logistical. Rocket payloads are optimized, transfer windows computed, and habitat masses minimized. This is necessary but deeply insufficient. Transportation solves the problem of initial delivery; it does not solve the problem of persistence. A settlement that depends indefinitely on terrestrial resupply remains an outpost whose productive capacity is bounded by what can be imported. A genuine civilization, by contrast, has its productive capacity bounded only by local energy flux and material availability. The distinction is not terminological but structural, and it has never been formally characterized.

The missing theoretical object is a rigorous account of industrial closure: the state in which a system can manufacture, from locally available resources, all components required for its own continued operation and expansion. The present dissertation provides a first-principles derivation of that account.

2. Replication, Reproduction, and the Technology Spiral

Prior literature oscillates between two concepts that must be cleanly separated before the mathematics can be constructed.

Self-replication is the production of an identical copy. It is the subject of von Neumann’s universal constructor theory and a large body of subsequent work on self-replicating robotic systems. The concept is elegant but practically restrictive: an exact copy imposes that the successor can be no more capable than its predecessor.

Developmental reproduction is the production of a more capable successor from the productive assets of the current generation. This is the strategy correctly identified by Metzger et al. as the viable path for lunar bootstrapping: begin with a sub-replicating system and evolve it toward closure, so that each generation manufactures something more advanced than itself. The system may never strictly self-replicate; it may continuously transcend itself, and this is theoretically desirable.

Industrial closure is the fixed-point condition connecting these frames: the moment at which the set of processes the system can execute locally becomes a superset of the processes required to reproduce the system itself. The Technology Spiral is the developmental trajectory from the initial seed to this fixed point.

The critical bridge to a rigorous theory comes from the Product Space framework of Hidalgo and Hausmann in economic complexity theory. Real economies diversify along paths of capability adjacency: they move into products that share precursor knowledge and tooling with what they already produce, and they cannot leap arbitrarily across the capability graph. A nascent off-Earth industrial system faces exactly this constraint. Bootstrapping is therefore formally a shortest-path problem over a metric space of industrial complexity—a problem of topology, not merely of mass-budget optimization.

