Part II — The Topological Engine

3. The Capability Adjacency Graph

Definition 3.1 (Industrial Process Node). Let $n_i \in \mathcal{N}$ denote a discrete industrial process: a reproducible operation that transforms a defined feedstock set $R_i$, using a tooling set $T_i$, to produce a specified output class. Examples include sintered regolith block production ($n_1$), ilmenite hydrogen reduction ($n_2$), molten regolith electrolysis ($n_3$), aluminum FFC-Cambridge process ($n_4$), thick-film vacuum-tube deposition ($n_5$), and solar-grade silicon purification ($n_6$).

Definition 3.2 (Feedstock Distribution). For a process $n_i$, we represent its raw material requirement as a normalized mass-fraction vector $\mathbf{p}_i \in \Delta^{|\mathcal{E}|-1}$ over a chosen elemental basis $\mathcal{E}$. For the lunar case we adopt the five-element basis $\mathcal{E} = \{\text{Fe}, \text{Ti}, \text{Al}, \text{O}, \text{Si}\}$, capturing the dominant ISRU-relevant chemistry. Each component $p_i(k)$ is the required mass fraction of element $k$ in the feedstock $R_i$.

The two primary geochemical reference seeds, calibrated from Apollo sample data and current ISRU simulant studies, are:

Element $k$ High-Ti Mare Basalt $\mathbf{p}_{\text{mare}}$ Highlands Anorthosite $\mathbf{p}_{\text{highlands}}$
Fe 0.15 0.05
Ti 0.08 0.01
Al 0.07 0.18
O 0.42 0.45
Si 0.28 0.31

These vectors serve as the origin nodes $n_0$ of the Technology Spiral for mare and highland deployment sites, respectively.

Definition 3.3 (Capability Proximity). Let process $n_i$ represent the current industrial state’s accessible elemental distribution (the substrate), and let $n_j$ be a candidate target process. The directed proximity $\phi_{ij} \in [0,1]$ from $n_i$ to $n_j$ is defined by

\phi_{ij} = \alpha \cdot \exp\!\Bigl(-D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathbf{p}_j \,\|\, \mathbf{p}_i)\Bigr) + (1-\alpha) \cdot \Bigl(1 - \frac{|T_i \,\triangle\, T_j|}{|T_i \cup T_j|}\Bigr),

where

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathbf{p}_j \,\|\, \mathbf{p}_i) = \sum_{k \in \mathcal{E}} p_j(k) \ln \frac{p_j(k)}{p_i(k)}

is the Kullback–Leibler divergence from the target feedstock to the substrate, and the Jaccard term measures the fraction of tooling requirements not shared between the two processes. The parameter $\alpha \in (0,1)$ weights material versus tooling proximity and is in principle empirically calibratable from terrestrial industrial transition datasets. We adopt the convention $0 \cdot \ln(0/q) = 0$ and $p \ln(p/0) = +\infty$ for $p>0$.

Theorem 3.1 (Infinite Penalty Property). If a target process $n_j$ requires a non-zero mass fraction of an element $k^* \in \mathcal{E}$ that is entirely absent from the current substrate—that is, $p_j(k^*) > 0$ while $p_i(k^*) = 0$—then $D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathbf{p}_j \,\|\, \mathbf{p}_i) \to +\infty$ and consequently $\phi_{ij} = 0$, rendering the capability distance $d(n_i,n_j) = +\infty$.

Proof. The term contributed by $k^*$ to the sum in $D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathbf{p}_j \,\|\, \mathbf{p}_i)$ is $p_j(k^*) \ln\bigl(p_j(k^*)/p_i(k^*)\bigr)$. With $p_i(k^*) = 0$ and $p_j(k^*) > 0$, the ratio diverges to $+\infty$, and the logarithm diverges linearly, making the entire sum infinite regardless of all other terms. The exponential factor then evaluates to zero, and because the Jaccard term is bounded in $[0,1]$, the convex combination yields $\phi_{ij}=0$. $\square$

Physical meaning. This theorem captures what engineers recognize as a “show-stopper”: a process that requires even trace quantities of an element absent from the local accessible material base cannot be executed, no matter how sophisticated the available tooling. The capability distance becomes literally infinite. The system is geometrically locked out. The only remedies are (a) importing the missing element as a chemical seed from Earth, or (b) selecting a landing site where the element is locally available—both of which can be modeled as contracting the infinite distance to a finite value by augmenting the substrate distribution $\mathbf{p}_i$.

Definition 3.4 (Capability Adjacency Matrix). $\mathbf{\Omega} \in [0,1]^{|\mathcal{N}| \times |\mathcal{N}|}$ is the matrix of pairwise proximities $\phi_{ij}$. The directed graph $\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{N}, \mathbf{\Omega})$ is the Industrial Capability Space of the given planetary environment.

Definition 3.5 (Technology Spiral). The Technology Spiral from seed $n_0$ is the sequence of expanding $k$-neighborhoods under capability distance:

n_0 \subset \mathcal{B}_1(n_0) \subset \mathcal{B}_2(n_0) \subset \cdots \subset \mathcal{B}^*(n_0),

where $\mathcal{B}_k(n_0)$ is the set of all processes reachable from $n_0$ by a directed path of length at most $k$ edges, each edge having finite proximity. The spiral terminates at the closure fixed-point.

Definition 3.6 (Industrial Closure). Let $\mathcal{R}(t)$ be the set of processes required to reproduce the current system state at time $t$. Closure is achieved at step $k^*$ when

\mathcal{R}(t) \subseteq \mathcal{B}_{k^*}(n_0).

The closure manifold $\mathcal{M}_C$ is the set of all system states satisfying this condition on $\mathcal{G}$.

4. The Gated Non-Linear State-Space Formulation

Definition 4.1 (Industrial State Vector). The metabolic state of the UCC at time $t$ is

\mathbf{x}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} E(t) \\ M(t) \\ I(t) \\ A(t) \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^4_{\ge 0},

where $E(t)$ is the net power surplus available for productive work (watts above metabolic upkeep), $M(t)$ is the Shannon entropy of the accessible processed material feedstocks (a diversity index over the operational elemental composition space), $I(t)$ is the total information processing capacity of the cognitive layer (bits per second of actionable planning output), and $A(t)$ is the automation depth (fraction of productive processes executable without real-time human intervention).

Definition 4.2 (Activation Gate). The metabolic gate function $\sigma : \mathbb{R}^2 \to [0,1]$ is defined as

\sigma(E, I) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp\!\bigl(-\beta_E(E - E_{\min})\bigr)} \cdot \frac{1}{1 + \exp\!\bigl(-\beta_I(I - I_{\min})\bigr)},

where $E_{\min}$ and $I_{\min}$ are the minimum energy flux and information bandwidth required to sustain the current infrastructure’s upkeep, and $\beta_E, \beta_I > 0$ are steepness parameters. $\sigma \to 0$ when either threshold is violated, and $\sigma \to 1$ when both are comfortably exceeded.

Theorem 4.1 (Gated State Evolution). The evolution of the industrial state vector under UCC dynamics is governed by

\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) = \sigma\!\bigl(E(t), I(t)\bigr) \cdot \mathbf{F}\!\bigl(\mathbf{K}_{\mathrm{inf}}(t),\, \mathbf{\Omega}\bigr) \cdot \mathbf{x}(t) \odot \mathbf{x}(t),

where $\mathbf{F}$ is the non-linear growth operator parameterized by the infrastructure footprint matrix $\mathbf{K}_{\mathrm{inf}}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{4 \times 4}$ and the capability adjacency matrix $\mathbf{\Omega}$, and $\odot$ denotes the Hadamard product.

Proof of Absolute Bottleneck. If $E(t) \to E_{\min}^-$ (or $I(t) \to I_{\min}^-$), then $\sigma(E,I) \to 0$. Because $\sigma$ multiplies the entire right-hand side before the Hadamard product, the time derivative vector approaches zero simultaneously in all components. Thus insufficient energy or information processing halts not only its own layer but the material processing, cognitive, and automation layers instantaneously. No off-diagonal element of $\mathbf{F}$ can circumvent the gate; the bottleneck is absolute. $\square$

Corollary 4.1 (Rate-Limiting Dimension). In the regime $\sigma \approx 1$, the dominant eigenvalue $\lambda_{\max}$ and corresponding eigenvector $\mathbf{v}_{\max}$ of $\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{K}_{\mathrm{inf}}, \mathbf{\Omega})$ identify the dimension of the state space that most strongly limits growth. Concentrating investment along $\mathbf{v}_{\max}$ yields maximum marginal expansion; investment orthogonal to it yields sub-linear returns.

Definition 4.3 (Strategic Catalysis). The strategic cognitive stratum $I_{\mathrm{strategic}}$—implemented on imported Earth-supplied radiation-hardened silicon—does not appear as a mass input in the material balance equations of $\mathbf{K}_{\mathrm{inf}}$. It enters solely as an efficiency multiplier on the growth operator:

\mathbf{F}\!\bigl(\mathbf{K}_{\mathrm{inf}}, \mathbf{\Omega}\bigr) = \mathbf{F}_0\!\bigl(\mathbf{K}_{\mathrm{inf}}, \mathbf{\Omega}\bigr) \cdot \gamma\!\bigl(I_{\mathrm{strategic}}\bigr),

where $\gamma : \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} \to [1, \gamma_{\max}]$ is a monotonically increasing saturation function with $\gamma(0) = 1$ (baseline efficiency) and $\gamma \to \gamma_{\max}$ as $I_{\mathrm{strategic}}$ saturates. In biochemical analogy, $I_{\mathrm{strategic}}$ is a catalytic enzyme: it accelerates the kinetics of the growth operator without appearing in the stoichiometric mass balance. It modifies how fast the system traverses the Technology Spiral; it does not modify which nodes are reachable—reachability is determined solely by $\mathbf{\Omega}$ and the feedstock distributions.

