Part III — The Thermodynamic Governor

5. Bounding Recursive Growth

Definition 5.1 (Maintenance Exergy Cost). Each process node $n \in \mathcal{N}$ that the system maintains in an operational state requires a continuous exergy input $\dot{X}_{\mathrm{maint}}(n)$ to counteract entropic degradation. The total maintenance load on the system is

\dot{X}_{\mathrm{maint}}(t) = \sum_{n \in \mathcal{A}(t)} \dot{X}_{\mathrm{maint}}(n),

where $\mathcal{A}(t)$ is the active process set at time $t$.

Theorem 5.1 (Thermodynamic Scaling Limit). The set of processes $\mathcal{A}(t)$ that the system can actively sustain is bounded by the net exergy flux:

\sum_{n \in \mathcal{A}(t)} \dot{X}_{\mathrm{maint}}(n) \le \dot{X}_{\mathrm{net}}(t) = \dot{X}_{\mathrm{input}}(t) - \dot{X}_{\mathrm{upkeep}}(t),

where $\dot{X}_{\mathrm{input}}(t)$ is the total primary exergy supply, $\dot{X}_{\mathrm{upkeep}}(t)$ is the irreducible metabolic cost of the infrastructure itself, and all terms are measured at the local thermal sink temperature $T_{\mathrm{sink}}$. Equivalently, the cardinality of the active process set obeys

|\mathcal{A}(t)| \le \frac{\dot{X}_{\mathrm{net}}(t)}{\min_{n \in \mathcal{A}(t)} \dot{X}_{\mathrm{maint}}(n)}.

Proof. Each maintained process dissipates exergy at a rate bounded below by its minimum maintenance cost. Since the total exergy available for all processes is $\dot{X}_{\mathrm{net}}(t)$, the number of simultaneously operable processes cannot exceed the given bound. The inequality is strict if maintenance costs are heterogeneous. $\square$

Corollary 5.1 (Closure Manifold as Attractor). The trajectory of the UCC is not an unbounded exponential but a finite curve terminating at the closure manifold $\mathcal{M}_C$—a stable attractor in capability space representing the maximum industrial complexity sustainable with the local thermodynamic resources. The Technology Spiral is the minimum-time geodesic from $n_0$ to $\mathcal{M}_C$ on $\mathcal{G}$, subject to the scaling limit.

Entropy generation accounting. The unavoidable entropy production rate $\dot{S}_{\mathrm{gen}}(t)$ comprises four irreducible contributions:

\dot{S}_{\mathrm{gen}}(t) = \dot{S}_{\mathrm{wear}}(t) + \dot{S}_{\mathrm{chem}}(t) + \dot{S}_{\mathrm{Landauer}}(t) + \dot{S}_{\mathrm{rad}}(t),

where $\dot{S}_{\mathrm{wear}}$ is tool-wear entropy from abrasive excavation and machining, $\dot{S}_{\mathrm{chem}}$ captures irreversible reaction entropy in smelting and reduction, $\dot{S}_{\mathrm{Landauer}}$ accounts for logical bit-erasures in the cognitive layer (at least $k_B T_{\mathrm{sink}} \ln 2$ per bit), and $\dot{S}_{\mathrm{rad}}$ is radiation-induced disordering from cosmic-ray flux. The total entropy must be rejected to the environment at $T_{\mathrm{sink}}$, placing a hard upper bound on the total useful work rate. The cognitive layer is thus thermodynamically non-free: its information metabolism binds Layer 5 (Cognition) to Layer 1 (Energy) at the level of fundamental physics. Intelligence has a power bill that cannot be waived.

6. Stochastic Industrial Drift and Fidelity Control

Manufacturing processes in deep space are subject to continuous stochastic perturbations: thermal cycling (temperature swings exceeding $280^\circ\mathrm{C}$ on the lunar surface), micrometeorite impact vibrations, and high-flux cosmic ray bombardment inducing both material fatigue and bit-flip errors in process control software. A purely deterministic treatment of manufacturing fidelity is physically inadequate.

Definition 6.1 (Industrial Drift). Let $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n$ be the vector of dimensional and geometric tolerance deviations of manufactured components from their canonical specifications at time $t$.

Theorem 6.1 (Stochastic Industrial Drift Dynamics). The evolution of the tolerance deviation vector is governed by the Itô stochastic differential equation

d\boldsymbol{\epsilon}(t) = \bigl(\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{drift}} \boldsymbol{\epsilon}(t) - \mathbf{B} \mathbf{u}(t)\bigr)\,dt + \mathbf{\Sigma}\,d\mathbf{W}(t),

where $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{drift}} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ encodes systematic tool-wear, thermal fatigue, and material creep; $\mathbf{B} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times m}$ is the control influence matrix; $\mathbf{u}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^m$ is the active correction input generated by the fidelity control protocol $\mathcal{C}$; $\mathbf{\Sigma} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ is the diffusion matrix encoding the intensity of unpredictable perturbations; and $d\mathbf{W}(t)$ is an $n$-dimensional standard Wiener process representing micrometeorite vibrations, cosmic-ray-induced bit flips propagating into process control, and unmodeled thermal transients.

Theorem 6.2 (Fidelity Stability Condition). The fidelity correction protocol $\mathcal{C}$ ensures bounded industrial drift if and only if there exists a positive definite Lyapunov function $V(\boldsymbol{\epsilon}) = \boldsymbol{\epsilon}^T \mathbf{P} \boldsymbol{\epsilon}$ (with $\mathbf{P} \succ 0$) satisfying the stochastic stability condition under the Dynkin operator $\mathcal{L}$:

\mathcal{L}V(\boldsymbol{\epsilon}) = \boldsymbol{\epsilon}^T\!\bigl(\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{closed}}^T \mathbf{P} + \mathbf{P} \mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{closed}}\bigr)\boldsymbol{\epsilon} + \operatorname{tr}\bigl(\mathbf{\Sigma}^T \mathbf{P} \mathbf{\Sigma}\bigr) \le -c \|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\|^2 + d,

where $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{closed}} = \mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{drift}} - \mathbf{B}\mathbf{K}$ is the closed-loop drift matrix under linear feedback control $\mathbf{u} = \mathbf{K}\boldsymbol{\epsilon}$, and $c, d > 0$ are constants. This condition guarantees mean-square ultimate boundedness:

\limsup_{t \to \infty} \mathbb{E}\!\bigl[\|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}(t)\|^2\bigr] \le \frac{d}{c} \equiv \epsilon_{\max}^2.

Proof sketch. Applying Itô’s lemma to $V(\boldsymbol{\epsilon}(t))$ and taking expectations, we obtain $d\mathbb{E}[V]/dt = \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L}V]$. Under the given inequality, the expected Lyapunov function is ultimately bounded by $d/c$, which bounds the mean-square deviation. The term $\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{\Sigma}^T \mathbf{P} \mathbf{\Sigma})$ represents the irreducible variance floor imposed by the Wiener noise—the fundamental lower bound on achievable fidelity. Perfect fidelity is thermodynamically prohibited; the correct engineering objective is drift bounded below the tolerance threshold $\epsilon_{\max}$ required for successful reproduction. $\square$

Corollary 6.1 (Irreducible Drift Floor). The minimum achievable mean-square tolerance deviation is

\epsilon_{\mathrm{floor}}^2 = \frac{\operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{\Sigma}^T \mathbf{P} \mathbf{\Sigma})}{c}.

This quantity is a function of the local radiation environment (which sets $\mathbf{\Sigma}$) and the quality of the metrological feedback system (which sets $\mathbf{K}$ and hence $c$). It establishes a planetary-environment-specific minimum viable manufacturing precision: a harsher radiation environment demands a more sophisticated fidelity correction system, consuming more energy from the thermodynamic budget and tightening the constraint of Theorem 5.1.

Definition 6.2 (Blueprint Archive Integrity). The fidelity correction protocol $\mathcal{C}$ requires a write-protected archive of canonical manufacturing specifications $\mathcal{D}^*$ preserved against radiation-induced corruption. With an error-correcting code capable of correcting up to $t_{\mathrm{corr}}$ errors, the corruption probability after time $\Delta t$ satisfies

P_{\mathrm{corruption}}(\Delta t) \le \binom{R}{t_{\mathrm{corr}}+1} \bigl(p_{\mathrm{flip}}(\Delta t)\bigr)^{t_{\mathrm{corr}}+1} \le \delta_{\mathrm{archive}},

where $p_{\mathrm{flip}}(\Delta t)$ is the per-bit cosmic-ray upset probability over the interval, $R$ is the size of the blueprint archive in bits, and $\delta_{\mathrm{archive}}$ is the acceptable corruption probability. As $R$ grows with industrial complexity, the required error-correction overhead grows proportionally, imposing an additional charge against the thermodynamic budget of Theorem 5.1.

Energy cost of control. The active correction input $\mathbf{u}$ requires control power $P_{\mathrm{ctrl}} = \mathbf{u}^T \mathbf{R} \mathbf{u}$ with $\mathbf{R} \succ 0$. Under the feedback law $\mathbf{u} = \mathbf{K}\boldsymbol{\epsilon}$, the steady-state expected control power is

\bar{P}_{\mathrm{ctrl}} = \operatorname{tr}\!\bigl(\mathbf{K}^T \mathbf{R} \mathbf{K} \, \mathbf{P}_{ss}\bigr),

where $\mathbf{P}_{ss}$ is the steady-state covariance matrix of $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}$. The total exergy demand of the fidelity control layer therefore scales with the environmental noise intensity and the required precision, directly linking the tolerance bound to the energy condition.

