7. Functional Gradients of Cognition and the Electronics Phase Transition

Definition 7.1 (Cognitive Stratum Partition). The cognitive state $I(t)$ is partitioned into two parallel substrata:

I(t) = I_{\mathrm{local}}(t) + I_{\mathrm{strategic}}(t).

Stratum Function Physical Substrate Position in $\mathcal{G}$
$I_{\mathrm{local}}$ Motion control, sensor fusion, process sequencing, thermal regulation Thick-film macro-electronics, analog fluidic logic, vacuum-tube circuits Early-phase $\mathcal{B}_k(n_0)$: manufacturable via sintering and basic deposition
$I_{\mathrm{strategic}}$ Capability graph optimization, blueprint archiving, Earth communications, anomaly detection Radiation-hardened silicon CMOS with triple-modular redundancy Pre-closure: imported Earth seed; post-transition $t^*$: locally manufacturable

Theorem 7.1 (Decoupled Cognitive Closure). The closure fixed-point condition (Definition 3.6) can be satisfied without locally manufacturing $I_{\mathrm{strategic}}$ hardware, provided

\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{system}}(t) \setminus \{I_{\mathrm{strategic}}\} \subseteq \mathcal{B}_{k^*}(n_0),

and the strategic stratum is maintained through the catalytic enzyme model of Definition 4.3, with imported hardware mass flow satisfying a small threshold fraction of total system mass.

Proof. Because $I_{\mathrm{strategic}}$ does not enter the material feedstock matrices of $\mathbf{K}_{\mathrm{inf}}$—it only modulates the growth operator via $\gamma(I_{\mathrm{strategic}})$—its absence from the locally manufactured set does not invalidate material closure. The fixed point $\mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathcal{B}_{k^*}(n_0)$ can be met by the remaining processes; only the rate of approach, not the reachability, is affected. $\square$

Theorem 7.2 (Electronics Phase Transition). There exists a critical time $t^*$ in the Technology Spiral at which the locally manufacturable computing substrates of $I_{\mathrm{local}}$ achieve sufficient information density to bootstrap the next tier of electronic complexity—specifically, the production of rudimentary photolithographic masks and doped semiconductor substrates using locally produced ultra-pure silicon, local vacuum deposition equipment, and basic clean-room atmospheric control.

The location of $t^*$ on the Technology Spiral is proportional to the geodesic distance in $\mathcal{G}$ from thick-film electronics node $n_5$ to thin-film silicon deposition node $n_6$:

t^* \propto d(n_5, n_6) = \min_{\text{paths}} \sum_{\text{edges } (i,j)} (1 - \phi_{ij}),

where the sum runs over the minimum-cost directed path in $\mathcal{G}$ from $n_5$ to $n_6$.

The Silicon Chasm. The transition from metallurgical-grade silicon (a natural output of molten regolith electrolysis at $>1600^\circ\mathrm{C}$) to solar-grade silicon ($99.9999\%$ purity via Siemens or fluidized-bed CVD) to electronic-grade silicon ($99.9999999\%$ purity plus dopant precision) involves sequential leaps in the capability graph. The critical obstacle is the requirement for dopant elements (e.g., phosphorus, boron) that are absent from the five-element lunar basis $\mathcal{E}$. Let $n_7$ be the doped electronic silicon process node, with feedstock distribution $\mathbf{p}_7$ containing $p_7(\text{P}) > 0$. Since the lunar substrate $\mathbf{p}_{\text{mare}}$ has $p_{\text{mare}}(\text{P}) = 0$, Theorem 3.1 forces $\phi_{\text{mare},7} = 0$: an infinite capability distance. The phase transition at $t^*$ is therefore gated by an Earth-imported chemical seed of precisely these trace reagents—a small, high-value catalytic cargo that collapses the infinite distance to a finite traversable step by augmenting the substrate distribution with the missing element.

