Part V — Synthesis and Numerical Foundations

8. Necessary and Sufficient Conditions for Civilizational Reproduction

Theorem 8.1 (UCC Existence Theorem). A physical seed system $\mathcal{S}_0$ deployed on a planetary body $\mathcal{P}$ will converge to industrial closure if and only if all four of the following conditions are simultaneously satisfied:

(C1) Energy Condition. The net exergy flux of the initial seed must exceed the combined maintenance power of the seed infrastructure and the minimum control power required to keep industrial drift bounded:

\dot{X}_{\mathrm{net}}(0) > \dot{X}_{\mathrm{maint}}(\mathcal{S}_0) + \bar{P}_{\mathrm{ctrl}}(\epsilon_{\max}),

where $\bar{P}_{\mathrm{ctrl}}(\epsilon_{\max})$ is the steady-state control power derived from Theorem 6.2 for the target tolerance $\epsilon_{\max}$.

(C2) Adjacency Condition. The seed node $n_0$ must lie at finite capability distance from the closure-reachable set $\mathcal{M}_C$:

d(n_0, \mathcal{M}_C) < \infty,

equivalently, no process in $\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{system}}$ requires a feedstock constituent entirely absent from the planetary body and unreachable via a finite imported chemical seed.

(C3) Fidelity Condition. The cognitive layer must implement a fidelity correction protocol $\mathcal{C}$ satisfying the Lyapunov stability condition of Theorem 6.2, guaranteeing

\limsup_{t \to \infty} \mathbb{E}\bigl[\|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}(t)\|^2\bigr] \le \epsilon_{\max}^2.

(C4) Thermodynamic Feasibility Condition. The total exergy investment $W_{\mathrm{closure}}$ required to traverse the Technology Spiral from $n_0$ to $\mathcal{M}_C$ must be less than the integrated net exergy over the available time horizon, and the peak entropy generation rate must remain rejectable at the local sink temperature:

\int_{0}^{t_{\mathrm{close}}} \dot{X}_{\mathrm{net}}(t)\,dt \ge W_{\mathrm{closure}}, \qquad \max_{t \in [0,t_{\mathrm{close}}]} \dot{S}_{\mathrm{gen}}(t) \le \frac{\dot{X}_{\mathrm{input}}(t) - \dot{X}_{\mathrm{upkeep}}(t)}{T_{\mathrm{sink}}}.

These four conditions are universal. They are independent of specific technology epoch, planetary chemistry, robotic architecture, or economic organization. Any technological system, anywhere in the universe, that attempts to reproduce industrial civilization in a new environment must satisfy all four. They constitute the laws of civilizational reproduction.

