Appendix A: Mathematical Preliminaries

This appendix reviews the mathematical tools used in the dissertation, establishing notation and key results.

A.1 Kullback–Leibler Divergence

For two discrete probability vectors $\mathbf{p}, \mathbf{q} \in \Delta^{d-1}$ with $\mathbf{q} \succ 0$ (strict positivity), the Kullback–Leibler divergence is

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathbf{p} \,\|\, \mathbf{q}) = \sum_{k=1}^d p_k \ln \frac{p_k}{q_k}.

We extend the definition to allow $\mathbf{q}$ with zero entries using the conventions $0 \ln \frac{0}{0} = 0$, $0 \ln \frac{0}{q_k} = 0$ for $q_k > 0$, and $p_k \ln \frac{p_k}{0} = +\infty$ for $p_k > 0$. Under these conventions, $D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\mathbf{p} \,\|\, \mathbf{q}) < \infty$ if and only if $\operatorname{supp}(\mathbf{p}) \subseteq \operatorname{supp}(\mathbf{q})$.

A.2 Directed Graphs and Shortest Paths

A directed graph $\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{N},\mathbf{\Omega})$ with node set $\mathcal{N}$ and adjacency matrix $\mathbf{\Omega} \in [0,1]^{|\mathcal{N}|\times|\mathcal{N}|}$ has edge weights $w_{ij} = 1 - \phi_{ij}$ if $\phi_{ij} > 0$, and $w_{ij} = \infty$ if $\phi_{ij}=0$. The shortest-path distance $d(n_i,n_j)$ is the minimum total weight over all directed paths. By Theorem 3.1, $w_{ij}=\infty$ exactly when a required element is missing.

A.3 Stochastic Differential Equations and Lyapunov Stability

Consider the Itô SDE

d\mathbf{X}(t) = \mathbf{a}(\mathbf{X})\,dt + \mathbf{B}(\mathbf{X})\,d\mathbf{W}(t),

with $\mathbf{W}$ an $m$-dimensional Wiener process. For a twice continuously differentiable function $V:\mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$, the Dynkin operator (infinitesimal generator) is

\mathcal{L}V(\mathbf{x}) = \nabla V(\mathbf{x}) \cdot \mathbf{a}(\mathbf{x}) + \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{tr}\!\bigl[\mathbf{B}(\mathbf{x})^T \mathbf{H}_V(\mathbf{x}) \mathbf{B}(\mathbf{x})\bigr],

where $\mathbf{H}_V$ is the Hessian of $V$. For the linear case $\mathbf{a}(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{x}$, $\mathbf{B}$ constant, and $V(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{x}^T \mathbf{P} \mathbf{x}$ with $\mathbf{P} \succ 0$, we obtain

\mathcal{L}V(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{x}^T(\mathbf{A}^T \mathbf{P} + \mathbf{P}\mathbf{A})\mathbf{x} + \operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{B}^T \mathbf{P} \mathbf{B}).

A sufficient condition for mean‑square ultimate boundedness is that $\mathcal{L}V(\mathbf{x}) \le -c\|\mathbf{x}\|^2 + d$ with $c>0$, which yields $\limsup_{t\to\infty} \mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{X}(t)\|^2] \le d/c$.

