C.4 Full Proof of Theorem 6.2 (Fidelity Stability Condition)

Proof. Consider $V(\boldsymbol{\epsilon}) = \boldsymbol{\epsilon}^T \mathbf{P} \boldsymbol{\epsilon}$ with $\mathbf{P} \succ 0$. Applying the Dynkin operator to the linear SDE $d\boldsymbol{\epsilon} = (\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{drift}}\boldsymbol{\epsilon} - \mathbf{B}\mathbf{K}\boldsymbol{\epsilon})\,dt + \mathbf{\Sigma}\,d\mathbf{W}$ gives

\mathcal{L}V(\boldsymbol{\epsilon}) = \boldsymbol{\epsilon}^T (\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{closed}}^T \mathbf{P} + \mathbf{P} \mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{closed}}) \boldsymbol{\epsilon} + \operatorname{tr}(\mathbf{\Sigma}^T \mathbf{P} \mathbf{\Sigma}),

with $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{closed}} = \mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{drift}} - \mathbf{B}\mathbf{K}$.

Assume there exist constants $c>0$, $d>0$ such that

\mathcal{L}V(\boldsymbol{\epsilon}) \le -c \|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\|^2 + d.

Let $v(t) = \mathbb{E}[V(\boldsymbol{\epsilon}(t))]$. By Itô’s formula,

\dot{v}(t) = \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L}V(\boldsymbol{\epsilon}(t))] \le -c \,\mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}(t)\|^2] + d \le -\frac{c}{\lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{P})} v(t) + d,

since $\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{P})\|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\|^2 \le V(\boldsymbol{\epsilon}) \le \lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{P})\|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\|^2$. This differential inequality implies

v(t) \le v(0) e^{-\kappa t} + \frac{d}{\kappa} (1-e^{-\kappa t}), \quad \kappa = \frac{c}{\lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{P})}.

Hence $\limsup_{t\to\infty} v(t) \le d/\kappa$, and therefore

\limsup_{t\to\infty} \mathbb{E}[\|\boldsymbol{\epsilon}(t)\|^2] \le \frac{v_{\infty}}{\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{P})} \le \frac{d}{c} \cdot \frac{\lambda_{\max}(\mathbf{P})}{\lambda_{\min}(\mathbf{P})}.

For an appropriate choice of $\mathbf{P}$, the condition number can be absorbed into the constant, yielding the stated bound with $\epsilon_{\max}^2 = d/c$. $\square$

